From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 19:55:07 -0500
Subject: fix: keep the connections a launch opened ahead over https

A tab given on the command line connects ahead to its server, and its request
leaves a little later. If the certificate verifier announced a change in between,
the network service closed every idle socket of the profile and the tab opened a
new connection, which a server with a short accept queue can refuse; a background
tab then stays on the error. Patch 0077 stopped that for a verifier that has
verified nothing. Https tabs have made it verify something, and so has any https
page in a profile, so both still saw the flush, and http tabs in such a profile
with them.

The updates a launch sends change nothing the verifier answers. The profile's
certificates are the ones the service was made with, and the network time agrees
with the clock. So the same certificates again no longer reach the verifier, and
a time within the uncertainty the factory accepts of the clock the verifier was
going by rebuilds the verifier without telling the client. A real change still
tells it: other certificates, a CRLSet, a root store, a time further off.

The test server now tells connections by the client's port, since over TLS the
socket it reads is not the one it accepted, serves only those opened ahead once
told to, and waits for the client's second flight, which comes once it has
verified the certificate. CRLSetDoesNotDowngrade verifies first, as
CRLSetIsUpdatedIfNewer already did for 0077.

Why: tabs R46 (BACKLOG S-251). Https tabs, and http tabs after a verified
certificate, each failed on their server's second connection when an update came
between the connection ahead and the request.
Removable when: upstream stops telling clients of an update that changes nothing,
or stops closing idle sockets for it.
